00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:01.146
Those aren't the ones
I'm talking about.

00:03:01.146 --> 00:03:05.210
The five and 1/4-inch floppies,
those things held 360k

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:06.530
of storage.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:09.740
These microcontrollers hold
less than that, on average.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.240
If we remember
those floppy disks,

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.090
a single program
came on many disks.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.595
So to load an
application, we had

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:16.970
to switch those
things in and out

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.860
to load your program into RAM.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.300
We don't have that luxury
with microcontrollers.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.990
We can't just swap them in and
out to load an application.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:26.510
That's why we need something--
a more powerful computer,

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.280
or Cloud IoT Core--

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.220
to expand the capabilities
of the device.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.602
So since I keep
bringing it up, I

00:03:32.602 --> 00:03:35.060
want to make sure we're all on
the same page with IoT Core.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.280
What is IoT Core?

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.854
It's a 100% managed service.

00:03:39.854 --> 00:03:42.020
This means that you don't
have to stand up anything.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.450
You don't have to put
a service up in a VM.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.550
We handle all of that
for you in Google.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:49.280
There's no need for auto
scaling, redundancy, scaling.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.310
All of that is done
by the cloud for you.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:53.720
IoT Core provides
a global endpoint.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:55.940
No matter where your
devices are in the world,

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.110
it's the same endpoint.

00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:59.330
So latency is to your
nearest data center and back,

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.050
and that's it.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.200
IoT Core has two main pieces.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.470
It's a communication broker,
and it's a device management.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:10.580
For IoT Core, the organization
of it is you have IoT Core.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.230
Inside, you create registries.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.020
Registries are buckets
for your devices,

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.190
so you can create some
logical organization to them,

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.160
and on the devices yourselves.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.380
The messages from
your devices will--

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.500
using either HTTPS or
the MQTT protocol--

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.320
shuttle your device's
data up into the cloud.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.110
And then IoT Core brokers--

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.610
that's the communication
pieces-- your messages

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.062
from IoT Core into
Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:04:33.062 --> 00:04:34.520
and we'll talk
about Pub/Sub later.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.100
Pub/Subs are event
stream manager.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.900
And from there,
it's the gateway.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.060
IoT Core gives you
access to the rest

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.490
of Google Cloud's platform.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:46.190
So to wrap your head
around where we are now

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:47.930
and why it's really
getting excited,

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.140
I want to go back to our
little history lesson

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.700
and talk about how
electronics evolved.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.340
In the before times,
your household stuff,

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:56.960
like your blender,
your microwave,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.467
your washing
machine, all of these

00:04:58.467 --> 00:05:00.050
were made up of
electronic components,

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:03.200
like capacitors,
diodes, resistors.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.580
Things that would respond to
electric signal strength alone.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.247
The more powerful
the signal, the more

00:05:08.247 --> 00:05:10.080
it would happen in the
electrical component.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.570
So your microwave, for example.

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:14.960
How it works, the magnetron
inside your microwave,

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.405
you increase signal strength
to it, your food gets hotter.

00:05:17.405 --> 00:05:20.030
Decrease signal strength to it,
and it reduces the temperature.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:22.380
That's how basic
electronics work.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:25.490
Because of the
physical relationship

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.090
of these components
to each other,

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:29.570
as the electrical
signal come in,

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:32.010
each component would actuate
or not actuate the next piece

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:32.510
it's in.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.430
It's like a Rube
Goldberg machine.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.680
And because of the tight
physical relationship to it,

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.420
change was very difficult.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:39.980
If you wanted to
change functionality

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.450
of your electrical
circuit, you had

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:42.860
to make physical changes to it.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:44.540
You had to change your BOM,
your Bill of Materials,

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:46.460
so there would be cost
associated with it.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.792
The minute you add something
to an electrical circuit,

00:05:48.792 --> 00:05:50.000
your resistance goes haywire.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.550
You might have to change the
whole layout of your circuit.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:54.383
If you wanted to mass
produce your circuits,

00:05:54.383 --> 00:05:55.260
this is a problem.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.551
Once you've created your
tooling for your manufacturing

00:05:57.551 --> 00:05:59.900
line in your circuit, mass
producing the same template's

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:00.831
nice and easy.

00:06:00.831 --> 00:06:02.330
If you've introduced
change, now you

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:06.150
have to retool everything
to recreate your circuits.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:09.666
So it was very expensive
to make any kind of change.

00:06:09.666 --> 00:06:11.040
Some of these
kinds of circuits--

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.680
we call them the analog
circuits-- still exist today.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.760
But modern electronics-- and
why this is all exciting--

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:18.270
are a little bit different.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:19.950
Modern electronics
are still composed

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:22.650
of sensors, components,
actuators, all of these things

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.630
that still work with
electrical signals.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.420
But instead of the way
that we used to do it,

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:30.450
where signal strength was
what changed things, now

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:32.310
we have things called
digital protocols.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:34.410
Electrical signal will go
on and off very rapidly

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.960
in a predefined pattern,
a digital protocol,

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.020
that is understood by
another piece in the circuit.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:43.080
This means that we can
center our electronics

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.410
around this constrained device,
this microcontroller, which

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.890
is very good at reading signals
both analog and digital,

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.260
and speaking in this
digital protocol.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.360
The chip itself can
now create functional

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.610
change in your circuit
without having to physically

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.560
change anything around it.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.010
It's important because
they're inexpensive.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.230
Until relatively recently,
these microcontrollers

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.410
were pretty expensive.

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:07.290
But now, mass
market, I can go out

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.510
and buy a microcontroller
with Wi-Fi and Bluetooth

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:11.100
for less than $5.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:13.560
So adding these complex
circuits and creating

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:17.070
these complex circuits
is now very inexpensive.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:20.610
The trade-off, of course, is
that microcontrollers are still

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:21.859
relatively constrained.

00:07:21.859 --> 00:07:23.400
They don't have a
lot of memory, they

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:24.899
don't have a lot
of storage, so they

00:07:24.899 --> 00:07:27.930
need something else
to help them achieve

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:30.120
the capabilities that they're
not able to accomplish

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:30.996
on their own.

00:07:30.996 --> 00:07:32.370
So hopefully, this
gives you kind

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.040
of a backdrop for why
we're at this tipping

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.950
point with electronics where
cost and complexity have

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.566
arrived where we can
do things at scale.

00:07:40.566 --> 00:07:41.940
Being able to
create these cheap,

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.070
complex circuits
with microcontrollers

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:45.600
allows us to think
about creating

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:49.350
those cheap or inexpensive
complex smart devices that

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:51.120
are going to connect our world.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.100
So take a step, and let's
talk about smart devices.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.400
What is a smart device?

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.024
At the simplest,
a smart device is

00:07:58.024 --> 00:07:59.940
something that can
complete an objective, that

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.460
can do a task without
human intervention.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.290
I don't need to do
anything for it.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:07.470
Modern electronics
and the availability

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:11.340
of these microcontrollers
makes intelligent control

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.630
of these devices
economically feasible.

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:15.570
And you might notice
I'm really harping

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:18.420
on economically feasible
and inexpensive, and I am.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:21.240
If there's just one takeaway
that I want you to walk away

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:24.540
from this talk with, it's that
what a few years ago might not

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.090
have been possible because
of cost and complexity,

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:28.590
is easily achievable now.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:31.140
We have the tools now to
create these ecosystems

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:33.210
of smart devices.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:35.559
So smart devices, so
we can understand them.

00:08:35.559 --> 00:08:37.626
There's three basic
approaches to a smart device,

00:08:37.626 --> 00:08:39.000
and I'm holding
up three fingers.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.370
First, there's 100% on-device.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.990
Everything is contained and
done within your single device.

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:45.870
Something like
Google Home, where

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:48.430
I can use my voice as
the input to a device.

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:50.650
It uses speech-to-text
to convert to text.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:52.595
It uses natural
language processing

00:08:52.595 --> 00:08:54.720
to analyze what I really
asked for, because maybe I

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.130
didn't ask for the right thing.

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:58.170
And runs some kind
of task on-device,

00:08:58.170 --> 00:08:59.880
and it can accomplish
it all without me

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:01.590
having to do anything else.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.782
I have a spoken word,
and something happens.

00:09:03.782 --> 00:09:06.240
It might use the internet to
call out to do something else,

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.224
but that's part of the task
it's already accomplishing.

00:09:09.224 --> 00:09:11.140
The second, there's an
edge or border routing.

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:14.220
Sometimes we call it as the
radio protocol approach.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:17.280
For example, Nest has released
something called OpenThread.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:20.940
OpenThread allows your
device to call out

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:24.720
to another nearby device to take
advantage of its capabilities.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:27.180
So say you've got a device
that can do everything

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.560
except internet.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.060
But whatever the
task is, it needs

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.684
to be able to call
out to the internet.

00:09:31.684 --> 00:09:33.510
Well, with OpenThread,
it can reach out

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.060
to another device that's
nearby and connect it,

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:38.160
and borrow its internet
connection in order

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:39.480
to accomplish its task.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.580
It's still doing some
of the work on board,

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:44.340
but it's using these other
devices to finish off

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:46.290
what it needs to be done.

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:49.020
And lastly, a device can
delegate all of its control

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:50.706
to something else.

00:09:50.706 --> 00:09:52.830
A lot of the times, these
microcontrollers do that.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:56.940
So they will become a
very dumb but very fast

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.460
gatherer of information,
gatherer of data,

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:01.770
and then hand off all of
that IO to something else.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.140
A computer nearby, the
cloud, something else.

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:05.844
Firmata, made
famous by Arduino--

00:10:05.844 --> 00:10:07.260
that's the perfect
example of what

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:08.790
this is-- over a
serial wire, you

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:12.060
can delegate all of your
control, all of the information

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:14.400
that you've gathered on
one device to a computer,

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.149
to something else nearby.

00:10:16.149 --> 00:10:18.690
So what if I told you that the
computer that we're connecting

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:21.780
to, that is doing all
of this power for us,

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:24.810
didn't have to be
anywhere near the devices.

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.106
And that's why we're
back to IoT Core.

00:10:27.106 --> 00:10:28.980
This side, you can start
to think about that.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:32.820
Like the concept of Firmata,
IoT Core connects your device

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:33.780
to the cloud.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:37.290
It expands the capabilities
of this little device

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:38.790
and all of its data
it's collecting

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:40.569
to the power of the cloud.

00:10:40.569 --> 00:10:42.360
It gives your device
these new capabilities

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:44.392
without adding any complexity.

00:10:44.392 --> 00:10:45.850
You don't need more
local hardware.

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.100
You don't need anything local.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:50.820
All you need to be able to
do is talk to the cloud.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.340
So all of this so far, that
our history in electronics

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.140
are talking about
smart devices, is

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:57.510
to hammer this point
home where we're

00:10:57.510 --> 00:10:59.460
in a world where
creating devices

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:01.552
that talk to the internet,
it's not only easy,

00:11:01.552 --> 00:11:02.260
it's inexpensive.

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:04.500
Here's the inexpensive again.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:05.250
We can all do it.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.451
So I've touched on why the
cloud is integral to IoT.

00:11:08.451 --> 00:11:10.200
But just in case you
aren't convinced yet,

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.510
I want to show you a couple
of things to make sure

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:13.350
that you understand.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:15.160
Let's take a look at this graph.

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:17.310
This graph is all
of the oil and gas

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:19.200
fields in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.360
There's a little
over 1,300 of them.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:24.270
Each of these gas fields could
have potentially thousands

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:26.040
of sensors all streaming data.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.640
To keep our math simple,
let's say it's just 1,000.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.640
From there, let's say
that each of these sensors

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.410
is only streaming
100 bytes a second.

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:34.210
It's nothing.

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:35.400
100 bytes a second.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:38.710
Even if that's true, that's
137 megabytes a second.

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:40.470
It's just over eight
gigabytes a minute.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.941
It's 11 and 1/2 terabytes a day.

00:11:43.941 --> 00:11:46.440
You could manage that data if
you want, but I'm just saying,

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.510
there's a reason the
cloud was created.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:50.610
The cloud offloads all
of the infrastructure,

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:53.467
all of the overhead of
managing this big data.

00:11:53.467 --> 00:11:55.050
You don't have to
worry about backups,

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.350
you don't have to worry about
IT people to run your machines

00:11:58.350 --> 00:11:59.760
and make sure you've got uptime.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:01.470
The cloud handles all of this.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:03.691
The other point I want to
talk about is security.

00:12:03.691 --> 00:12:05.190
Some of you may
have heard about it.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.350
A couple of years ago, there was
a thing called the Mirai botnet

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:08.280
attack.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.950
This is where a bunch
of unsecure devices--

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:12.720
mostly routers and
stuff like that--

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.180
were hijacked and used
to attack the domain name

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:17.119
services of the internet, DYN.

00:12:17.119 --> 00:12:18.660
Took down huge swaths
of the internet

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:22.920
for a pretty good amount of
time in the internet scheme.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:25.530
Also, a temperature
sensor in a fish tank.

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:26.790
Last year, this happened.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:29.130
A temperature sensor in
a fish tank in a casino

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:30.330
was compromised.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:32.700
The hacker was able to, through
this temperature sensor,

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:35.100
pull out all of the personal
information of the casino's

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:37.500
high rollers, all through
a temperature sensor that

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:39.190
didn't happen to be secured.

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:41.610
So security is
massively important.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:44.170
If you're doing IoT, you
need to think about security.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:48.090
IoT Core registers all of your
devices with an SSL key pair,

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:51.540
and does all your encrypted
communication through TLS1.2

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:54.780
or later to ensure that all of
your devices are secure when

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:56.700
they're talking to the cloud.

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.980
So we understand now, hopefully,
why IoT Core is awesome.

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:01.020
But what else?

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:04.230
IoT Core is this gateway into
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:08.050
What does the Cloud Platform
have for you for IoT?

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:11.410
This is a small sampling of what
Google Cloud Platform offers.

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:15.610
If you want a really good time,
go to cloud.google.com/products

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:16.960
and start scrolling.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:19.090
And just keep scrolling,
and keep scrolling.

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:20.320
It's really impressive.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.070
There's enough products
on there that even

00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:24.150
we don't know what all
the products really do.

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:25.150
They're all specialized.

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.649
There's a ton of products that
will fit pretty much any need

00:13:27.649 --> 00:13:28.300
that you want.

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:29.800
But back to our sample.

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:32.770
So first, we've got our
devices bringing their data up

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:33.700
into GCP.

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:36.880
As I've talked about before,
these payloads, the messages,

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:42.460
and brokered as events into
Cloud Pub/Sub, our event stream

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:44.860
manager.

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:46.577
From there, once
it's in Pub/Sub,

00:13:46.577 --> 00:13:48.160
we have some products
that can respond

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.540
to our incoming messages.

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.144
We've got Cloud Functions.

00:13:51.144 --> 00:13:52.810
Cloud Functions are
serverless functions

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:55.530
that just exist in
the serverless space

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:57.280
that you can call
either with an endpoint,

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:59.170
like you're calling
a URL, or they can

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:02.860
respond to events within GCP.

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:06.370
In our case, the Pub/Sub
message coming in

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:08.749
will trigger a Cloud
Function to run on it.

00:14:08.749 --> 00:14:11.290
The other product we've got on
here is called Cloud Dataflow.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:13.360
Dataflow is our managed
service to handle

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:15.400
transforming incoming streams.

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:18.010
It's managed, so again,
like Cloud IoT Core,

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:20.020
it will scale without you
having to do anything.

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:21.730
However much data
it's processing,

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:23.380
it will scale to handle it.

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:26.654
It can be used to do things
like filter incoming data,

00:14:26.654 --> 00:14:28.570
if there is only some
data you want to make it

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:29.950
into your final storage.

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:32.590
Or it can massage the data
into a specific format

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:35.200
if you need it to be
processed in a specific way.

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:37.710
It also acts as a
pipeline for your data.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:39.010
Cloud Pub/Sub is temporary.

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:40.840
Messages will stay
there for seven days.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:42.610
If you don't do something
with them, they go away.

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:44.110
You don't want your
data to go away.

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:46.540
So a product like Dataflow
can shuttle your data

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:50.270
into the other products,
which brings us to our storage

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:51.450
and analytics section.

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:53.270
There's way more
than are shown here.

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:55.240
These three are just
a very small sampling

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:55.990
of what we've got.

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:58.180
First, we have Bigtable.

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:02.740
Bigtable is our NoSQL
database option.

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:04.030
Again, manage service.

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:07.330
It will scale with however
much data you put in it.

00:15:07.330 --> 00:15:10.480
BigQuery is our enterprise-level
data warehousing.

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:14.650
It allows you to query your
big data, petabytes of data,

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:16.020
with simple SQL statements.

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:18.670
That means your data analysts
and your data scientists

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:20.200
don't have to
learn a new system.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.690
They can just use SQL to find
insights within your data.

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:25.060
They can spend their
time analyzing data,

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:26.694
not learning a new system.

00:15:26.694 --> 00:15:28.360
And finally, we've
got machine learning.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:30.100
TensorFlow is the
open source software

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:32.230
that allows you to
train your models

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:34.090
and run inference
against your models.

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:36.520
Cloud Machine Learning
engine is hosted TensorFlow.

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:39.670
It allows you to harness all of
the power of Google's backbone

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:41.440
to train and run
inference on your models

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:43.210
faster and more efficiently.

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:45.430
Finally, we have tools that
can visualize your data.

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:46.888
We've got a couple
of options here.

00:15:46.888 --> 00:15:48.130
We've got Cloud Datalab.

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:49.510
It's built on
Jupyter, so you can

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:51.970
build notebooks to visualize
the data however you want.

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:54.430
And we've got an out-of-the-box
solution, Data Studio,

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:56.770
which lets you look at time
slices of data in charts

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:59.660
and graphs, however you
want to visualize it.

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:01.910
So for IoT, with all
of these together,

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:04.600
you can do things like
perform device management

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:07.300
and monitor your health
across fleets of devices.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:11.120
You can warehouse and analyze
truly absurds amount of data.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:12.580
And lastly, you
can perform things

00:16:12.580 --> 00:16:15.850
like predictive maintenance in
manufacturing and utilities,

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:18.160
to see when your stuff is
going to break using tools

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:19.450
like Machine Learning.

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:22.180
Cloud IoT core
provides that gateway.

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:24.790
It provides the device
management connectivity

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:29.110
for all of your devices into the
rest of Google Cloud Platform.

00:16:29.110 --> 00:16:30.550
It's been only a year.

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:33.570
Last year at I/O was when
we announced Cloud IoT Core.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.835
So I want to take a
sec just to highlight

00:16:35.835 --> 00:16:36.960
what we've done since then.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:38.501
What are the
improvements we've made?

00:16:38.501 --> 00:16:41.290
First, when we announced
that beta a year ago,

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:43.150
it could only talk MQTT.

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.640
If you didn't have a
device that could do MQTT,

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:46.600
you're out of luck.

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:51.160
We've added HTTPS since then, so
now a broader range of devices

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:54.340
can connect to IoT Core and
have their data managed.

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:56.752
A year ago when we
launched, each registry--

00:16:56.752 --> 00:16:58.210
remember those
buckets of devices--

00:16:58.210 --> 00:17:02.110
mapped one-to-one
to the Pub/Sub.

00:17:02.110 --> 00:17:04.960
So any device data that was
coming into your registry

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:07.240
would only go into a
single bucket of Pub/Sub.

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:09.880
We've now added the ability
to map multiple Pub/Sub

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:11.440
topics to a single registry.

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:13.300
So you can shard
your data that way.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:16.510
You can organize your data
into multiple Pub/Sub topics.

00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:19.150
We've added
Stackdriver logging so

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:22.556
that you can monitor your health
through Stackdriver, which is--

00:17:22.556 --> 00:17:24.680
it's a logging and monitoring
tools that we've got.

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:26.896
I forgot to mention it before.

00:17:26.896 --> 00:17:29.020
And brand new-- and I don't
think anyone's actually

00:17:29.020 --> 00:17:30.910
announced this yet, so I'm kind
of excited I get to announce

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:31.840
something--

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:32.554
Android Things.

00:17:32.554 --> 00:17:34.720
I'm sure you've all heard
a lot about Android Things

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:36.010
across this conference.

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:38.260
Android Things has
created a plug-in

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:39.910
that they're releasing
next week that

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:42.076
will allow you to easily
connect your Android Things

00:17:42.076 --> 00:17:43.336
devices to IoT Core.

00:17:43.336 --> 00:17:44.710
So all of those
sessions that you

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:47.230
went to about Android Things,
and all the cool stuff

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:49.660
you can create using it, now
let that kind of percolate

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:50.912
in the back of your mind.

00:17:50.912 --> 00:17:52.120
What more can you do with it?

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:54.490
If it's connected to the
cloud, what else can you do?

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:57.820
What global reach can you
accomplish with Android Things

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:00.590
now that it can connect
to cloud IoT Core?

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:02.260
So now that I've
talked your ear off,

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:03.730
I'm going to bring
Gus back up, and he's

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:05.688
going to show you in
practice some of the stuff

00:18:05.688 --> 00:18:08.120
that I've been talking about.

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:09.149
GUS CLASS: Thanks, Gabe.

00:18:09.149 --> 00:18:10.690
Let's have round of
applause for Gabe

00:18:10.690 --> 00:18:12.292
and his wonderful blue hair.

00:18:12.292 --> 00:18:13.000
Look at this guy.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.900
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:18.182
Yeah, I don't know how
I'm going to follow that.

00:18:18.182 --> 00:18:19.140
Just kidding, actually.

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:21.600
I'll be the yin to his yang,
and I'll show you the things

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:24.640
that he's been talking about.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:29.190
So this is actually--
this is the third time

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:32.100
that I've shown the nuts
and bolts of how Cloud IoT

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:33.360
Core works.

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:37.500
And this is I/O right now,
and I remember a year ago,

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:39.750
the Cloud IoT Core team
came in front of you

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:45.010
all and demonstrated the
product for the first time.

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:48.270
And when they did
it, I have no idea

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:52.050
how they were able to get such
a robust and beautiful demo

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:55.350
at that point in time, because
there was a lot of pieces

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:58.477
that the experience was a cliff.

00:18:58.477 --> 00:19:00.060
The first time that
I showed this demo

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:05.130
that I'm just about to
do, it blew up in my face.

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:08.440
And that was for a
couple of reasons.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:12.390
One of them was we didn't have
the cloud console features

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:16.030
for IoT Core, so there
was no visual way

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:18.750
of adding devices and
creating registries.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:21.840
And there also was no
command line utilities

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:23.490
for managing IoT Core.

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:27.000
And so I had to write a
sample that would make API

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:31.110
calls with the device ID, the
registry ID, the project ID.

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:35.880
All these values that are
really easy to fat finger.

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:40.290
So when we went to
general availability,

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:44.407
the Google Cloud
SDK, or gcloud--

00:19:44.407 --> 00:19:45.990
for those who don't
know, Google Cloud

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:48.610
ships a command line utility
that's written in Python,

00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:49.290
I believe.

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:51.630
And it allows you
to do all the things

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:53.430
that you can do in
cloud programmatically

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:55.060
from the command line.

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:59.280
And it actually works really
well from cloud resources,

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:00.860
and if you go and
do the code lab,

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:04.170
I'm actually having you do a
lot of cloud resource management

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:06.300
using the gcloud SDK.

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:10.080
And so today, I'm
going to show you

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:13.080
how to do the nuts and bolts
of Cloud IoT Core just using

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:15.370
the cloud SDK as
opposed to using

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:17.610
the console, or
the command line,

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:20.070
or some API calls that
I've hacked together

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:22.300
in some sort of sample app.

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:25.540
So I'll just unlock my
[INAUDIBLE] machine,

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:28.210
and we'll switch to computer.

