mic-- ooh, my mic is on.

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:00.260
Do I need to get close to you?

00:35:05.677 --> 00:35:06.510
Can we use this mic?

00:35:09.765 --> 00:35:10.820
Oh, it's attached.

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:11.945
That's going to end poorly.

00:35:15.009 --> 00:35:18.805
[CHUCKLES] Do I just need to
get really close to you guys?

00:35:18.805 --> 00:35:19.755
GUS CLASS: All right.

00:35:19.755 --> 00:35:22.495
All right, I will speak to Gabe,
and Gabe will speak to you.

00:35:22.495 --> 00:35:24.786
GABE WEISS: It's going to be
a great game of telephone.

00:35:24.786 --> 00:35:26.190
GUS CLASS: OK.

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:27.600
So three weeks ago.

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:29.370
GABE WEISS: So three weeks ago.

00:35:29.370 --> 00:35:30.150
GUS CLASS: I.

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:30.816
GABE WEISS: Gus.

00:35:30.816 --> 00:35:32.080
[CHUCKLING]

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:32.580
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:35.460
GUS CLASS: Needed to do a demo.

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:36.970
GABE WEISS: Needed to do a demo.

00:35:36.970 --> 00:35:37.950
GUS CLASS: To this conference.

00:35:37.950 --> 00:35:38.625
GABE WEISS: To this conference.

00:35:38.625 --> 00:35:39.310
GUS CLASS: Yeah.

00:35:39.310 --> 00:35:39.540
GABE WEISS: Yeah.

00:35:39.540 --> 00:35:40.748
GUS CLASS: And I had nothing.

00:35:40.748 --> 00:35:43.236
GABE WEISS: He had nothing.

00:35:43.236 --> 00:35:44.610
So he went to the
sound guy who's

00:35:44.610 --> 00:35:46.158
getting him a new battery pack.

00:35:46.158 --> 00:35:47.790
GUS CLASS: And I had
lights, actually.

00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:48.600
GABE WEISS: He had some lights.

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:50.725
He had some projects he
was working with with LEDs.

00:35:50.725 --> 00:35:51.650
GUS CLASS: And, uh--

00:35:51.650 --> 00:35:52.540
oh, we're back!

00:35:52.540 --> 00:35:53.040
Yeah!

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:53.910
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:55.280
Let's hear it for the sound guy.

00:35:55.280 --> 00:35:55.780
All right.

00:35:55.780 --> 00:35:57.540
And so yeah, I had lights.

00:35:57.540 --> 00:35:59.280
And I thought you all
would be much more

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:01.680
excited if I showed you robots.

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:04.830
And so I had this toy,
it's a toy like this.

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:06.790
I want to show
you it under here.

00:36:06.790 --> 00:36:07.860
So it's this toy.

00:36:07.860 --> 00:36:09.240
And this little toy has--

00:36:09.240 --> 00:36:12.870
remember Gabe was talking about
digital systems, on and offs.

00:36:12.870 --> 00:36:14.360
Well, this toy has something--

00:36:14.360 --> 00:36:15.390
I saw this on this toy.

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:18.855
It's got an infrared receiver.

00:36:18.855 --> 00:36:22.590
And it has this little
remote associated

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:25.800
with it, which I have smudged
off anything that will

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:27.480
hint at what it actually is.

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:35.460
But this little toy responds
to a blinking light on here.

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:37.380
And I just showed
you a blinking light.

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:39.930
So what if instead
of that little light,

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:42.690
these signals, these
configuration messages that

00:36:42.690 --> 00:36:45.840
are transmitted to the devices,
are then instead interpreted

00:36:45.840 --> 00:36:49.290
into the motion commands
for this little toy?

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:51.220
And I thought that was cool.

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:56.670
And so I started prototyping
on boards very similar

00:36:56.670 --> 00:37:00.630
to that other one I showed
you, and I came up with this.

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:02.940
My friend added some
flair and some character

00:37:02.940 --> 00:37:04.560
to this little guy.

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:10.830
And so what happens is, when
the device comes online,

00:37:10.830 --> 00:37:12.080
it should wiggle a little bit.

00:37:15.280 --> 00:37:19.644
And that way, I will know
it's connected to the Wi-Fi.

00:37:19.644 --> 00:37:21.060
So there's a little
blinking light

00:37:21.060 --> 00:37:25.680
that indicates the various
stages of the boot.

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:30.070
And I'm going to
start a Flask app.

00:37:30.070 --> 00:37:32.280
All right, there it is.

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:36.300
So when it just nods, it
nodded its little head there.

00:37:36.300 --> 00:37:40.890
What was going on was it's
now connected to Wi-Fi.

00:37:40.890 --> 00:37:42.010
Oh.

00:37:42.010 --> 00:37:43.260
GABE WEISS: You do your thing.

00:37:43.260 --> 00:37:43.843
GUS CLASS: OK.

00:37:43.843 --> 00:37:46.020
It's now connected to Wi-Fi.

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:49.110
And-- this is not the device.

00:37:49.110 --> 00:37:52.470
And now I can go into my
device registry here again,

00:37:52.470 --> 00:37:55.360
and I can send configuration
messages and then control this.

00:37:55.360 --> 00:37:58.830
And what's interesting
is that no longer

00:37:58.830 --> 00:38:01.260
is it something that's like
a device that's locally here.

00:38:01.260 --> 00:38:03.370
This device could be anywhere.

00:38:03.370 --> 00:38:05.047
And it doesn't really--

00:38:05.047 --> 00:38:07.380
and at the end there could
be millions of these devices,

00:38:07.380 --> 00:38:09.120
and you can do things
like coordinate

00:38:09.120 --> 00:38:10.960
across those devices.

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:13.260
Anyway, so let's send
a configuration message

00:38:13.260 --> 00:38:16.162
to device that will cause
the device to turn its head.

00:38:16.162 --> 00:38:18.120
And so when the device
responds to the command,

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:21.050
it should just nudge
a little bit there.

00:38:21.050 --> 00:38:22.902
There it is, OK.

00:38:22.902 --> 00:38:24.360
And so what you'll
also notice that

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:28.380
on the front of this device,
I have added a sonar sensor.

00:38:28.380 --> 00:38:30.960
And that's sonar sensor will
pretty accurately measure

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:33.600
distance up to about
30 meters away.

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:36.060
And so let's just do--

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:38.550
I wanted to do something
that shows getting data

00:38:38.550 --> 00:38:41.490
from your devices, putting
that data into the cloud,

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:42.970
and then processing that data.

00:38:42.970 --> 00:38:46.740
So very similar to what
we do in the code lab.

00:38:46.740 --> 00:38:51.360
And so I created that
little Flask app.

00:38:53.970 --> 00:38:55.640
And then this
little Flask app has

00:38:55.640 --> 00:38:58.300
this UI that only a
developer could love.

00:38:58.300 --> 00:39:02.270
But when I click that button,
it sends a configuration message

00:39:02.270 --> 00:39:06.840
to this device, if I'm lucky.

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:09.030
If it's not cached.

00:39:09.030 --> 00:39:09.990
Let's see.

00:39:14.100 --> 00:39:16.310
And that configuration
message will

00:39:16.310 --> 00:39:21.260
trigger this device to start
moving in this sonar pattern.

00:39:21.260 --> 00:39:24.470
And these are toys,
so it's not going

00:39:24.470 --> 00:39:27.680
to turn exactly the same
amount of degrees every time,

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:29.180
but it's close
enough that we can

00:39:29.180 --> 00:39:32.600
get a general idea for the
surroundings around this robot.

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:38.120
And then on the blazing fast
internet to my computer,

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:39.140
it should render--

00:39:39.140 --> 00:39:41.360
oh, that's nice.

00:39:41.360 --> 00:39:42.740
OK.

00:39:42.740 --> 00:39:43.715
Still good, still good.

00:39:43.715 --> 00:39:44.670
All right.

00:39:44.670 --> 00:39:45.890
And so then we render--

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:47.300
it's all right, you can clap.

00:39:47.300 --> 00:39:51.110
So then we render a graph of
the surroundings of this device.

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:55.940
And so adding the internet
capabilities to this toy

00:39:55.940 --> 00:39:59.000
was as easy as just
adding a hat on top of it.

00:39:59.000 --> 00:40:04.130
And also, because of
the low requirements

00:40:04.130 --> 00:40:07.220
for the hardware
for Cloud IoT Core,

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:10.900
I'm able to build these hats
at a the cost of maybe just $5.

00:40:10.900 --> 00:40:14.090
And so you can imagine in your
factory, or in your store,

00:40:14.090 --> 00:40:16.970
or in your office,
or wherever you

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:19.712
have these modern
electronic systems,

00:40:19.712 --> 00:40:21.920
that you can communicate
with with internet-connected

00:40:21.920 --> 00:40:24.290
devices, then you can
add internet capabilities

00:40:24.290 --> 00:40:25.440
to those devices.

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:27.140
And because they're
so inexpensive,

00:40:27.140 --> 00:40:30.890
we actually no longer
have to show my laptop.

00:40:30.890 --> 00:40:33.800
It's actually
possible to produce

00:40:33.800 --> 00:40:37.010
a large number of these devices
in a relatively short period

00:40:37.010 --> 00:40:41.120
of time and then have
some fun with them.

00:40:41.120 --> 00:40:43.894
So sometime after
this conference,

00:40:43.894 --> 00:40:45.810
I'm going to try to open
source this software,

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:48.351
so if you all want to make your
own little internet-connected

00:40:48.351 --> 00:40:50.880
toys and get an idea as to how
these kinds of things work,

00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:52.350
you can check it out.

00:40:52.350 --> 00:40:55.850
And for now, I'm going to do
the thing that everyone wants

00:40:55.850 --> 00:40:57.560
me to do whenever I
show them the robots,

00:40:57.560 --> 00:41:01.190
and that is make them
move as a swarm army.

00:41:01.190 --> 00:41:03.650
OK.

00:41:03.650 --> 00:41:07.370
And it's at times like
this that I really

00:41:07.370 --> 00:41:12.080
appreciate how little data
the Cloud IoT Core product

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:15.440
sips, because I am totally not
using my phone for connecting

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:16.342
these devices.

00:41:16.342 --> 00:41:17.300
GABE WEISS: Totally is.

00:41:17.300 --> 00:41:19.220
GUS CLASS: [CHUCKLES] Yeah.

00:41:19.220 --> 00:41:20.180
All right.

00:41:23.865 --> 00:41:25.990
GABE WEISS: You should have
a bunch of them online.

00:41:25.990 --> 00:41:27.120
GUS CLASS: There we go.

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:27.620
OK.

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:34.900
And it's just about
time for them to march.

00:41:34.900 --> 00:41:35.400
Oops.

00:41:39.756 --> 00:41:42.030
All right.

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:43.160
And we'll go back to--

00:41:47.060 --> 00:41:51.010
and thank you, Gabe, for
wrangling all these robots.

00:41:51.010 --> 00:41:54.660
All right, [INAUDIBLE]-- uh oh.

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:57.460
Look out.

00:41:57.460 --> 00:41:59.870
Go, robots, go!

00:41:59.870 --> 00:42:01.640
GABE WEISS: It's the Wi-Fi.

00:42:01.640 --> 00:42:03.900
GUS CLASS: It's
probably the Wi-Fi.

00:42:03.900 --> 00:42:05.485
But, I mean--

00:42:05.485 --> 00:42:07.610
GABE WEISS: They're blinking
really pretty, though.

00:42:07.610 --> 00:42:10.930
GUS CLASS: They are
blinking really pretty.

00:42:10.930 --> 00:42:12.180
I feel pretty good about that.

00:42:12.180 --> 00:42:13.630
GABE WEISS: [CHUCKLES] Ooh!

00:42:13.630 --> 00:42:15.170
GUS CLASS: There they go, yeah!

00:42:15.170 --> 00:42:16.251
So there it is!

00:42:16.251 --> 00:42:16.750
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:16.750 --> 00:42:17.250
Thank you.

00:42:20.204 --> 00:42:23.800
GABE WEISS: [CHUCKLES]
We have a jumper.

00:42:23.800 --> 00:42:25.450
GUS CLASS: Uh-oh, uh-oh.

00:42:25.450 --> 00:42:26.139
All right.

00:42:26.139 --> 00:42:27.930
All right, they've
already become sentient.

00:42:27.930 --> 00:42:29.515
This one's trying to break free.

00:42:29.515 --> 00:42:32.485
All right.

00:42:32.485 --> 00:42:34.617
All right.

00:42:34.617 --> 00:42:35.950
AUDIENCE: Oh, there's some more!

00:42:35.950 --> 00:42:38.930
GUS CLASS: Yeah!

00:42:38.930 --> 00:42:39.430
Yes.

00:42:39.430 --> 00:42:40.054
GABE WEISS: OK.

00:42:40.054 --> 00:42:41.800
GUS CLASS: I also
wanted to do this just

00:42:41.800 --> 00:42:44.082
to make Gabe have to
dance around on stage.

00:42:44.082 --> 00:42:45.790
All right, so let's
go back to the slides

00:42:45.790 --> 00:42:46.748
and we'll just wrap up.

00:42:50.931 --> 00:42:51.430
OK.

00:42:54.700 --> 00:42:58.332
So going back to
other approaches

00:42:58.332 --> 00:43:04.030
to adding smart and intelligent
capabilities to your devices,

00:43:04.030 --> 00:43:06.400
I want to again say
there's other ways of doing

00:43:06.400 --> 00:43:08.720
this than just complete
delegation to the cloud.

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:11.290
You can use edge
board or routing

00:43:11.290 --> 00:43:13.630
to extend the capabilities
of nearby devices

00:43:13.630 --> 00:43:15.340
to other devices.

00:43:15.340 --> 00:43:18.880
You can use on-device smarts
with things like Android Things

00:43:18.880 --> 00:43:21.910
so if you have all the
capabilities without the cloud,

00:43:21.910 --> 00:43:24.300
or some capabilities
shared with the cloud,

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:26.320
Android Things is a really
great way to do this.

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:30.950
And also, as Gabe mentioned
at the very beginning,

00:43:30.950 --> 00:43:32.540
we have Stackdriver logging.

00:43:32.540 --> 00:43:34.810
And Stackdriver logging,
for some people,

00:43:34.810 --> 00:43:36.550
can get them a
lot of information

00:43:36.550 --> 00:43:39.790
about things like security
audits and other--

00:43:39.790 --> 00:43:41.920
and that combined with
the metadata of devices

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:45.340
may be enough for a lot of
what people's IoT needs are.

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:50.220
And because we went
a little bit over,

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:53.097
I will try to wrap up quickly,
and I'll let Gabe close us out.

00:43:53.097 --> 00:43:54.930
GABE WEISS: So we want
to hear from you, how

00:43:54.930 --> 00:43:56.770
you thought about this talk.

00:43:56.770 --> 00:44:00.460
So please go here and enter
in our talk, the Cloud IoT

00:44:00.460 --> 00:44:01.790
Overview.

00:44:01.790 --> 00:44:02.840
Thank you for coming.

00:44:02.840 --> 00:44:03.270
If you want to--

00:44:03.270 --> 00:44:03.770
[APPLAUSE]

